<div>
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="mb-12">
        <nav class="flex items-center gap-2 text-sm mb-4">
            <a href="/docs/builtins" class="text-gray-400 hover:text-white transition-colors">Built-ins</a>
            <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-gray-600" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 5l7 7-7 7" />
            </svg>
            <span class="text-white">HTTP</span>
        </nav>
        <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-white sm:text-5xl mb-6">
            <span class="bg-gradient-to-r from-green-400 to-emerald-400 bg-clip-text text-transparent">HTTP Class</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-8">
            HTTP client class for GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE requests with JSON support and parallel requests.
        </p>
    </div>

    <!-- HTTP Class Overview -->
    <section id="section-class-overview" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4 flex items-center gap-3">
            <span class="w-8 h-8 rounded-lg bg-green-500/20 flex items-center justify-center">
                <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-green-400" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                    <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 11H5m14 0a2 2 0 012 2v6a2 2 0 01-2 2H5a2 2 0 01-2-2v-6a2 2 0 012-2m14 0V9a2 2 0 00-2-2M5 11V9a2 2 0 012-2m0 0V5a2 2 0 012-2h6a2 2 0 012 2v2M7 7h10" />
                </svg>
            </span>
            HTTP Class
        </h2>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">All HTTP functions are accessed via the <code class="text-amber-400">HTTP</code> class:</p>
        <pre data-filename="Example"><code class="language-soli text-sm">response = HTTP.get("https://api.example.com/users")
response = HTTP.post("https://api.example.com/users", { "name": "Alice" })
response = HTTP.request("DELETE", "https://api.example.com/users/1")
stream = HTTP.stream("GET", "https://api.example.com/events")   # read line by line</code></pre>
    </section>

    <!-- Security: SSRF blocklist & redirects -->
    <section id="section-ssrf" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-12">
        <div class="rounded-xl bg-rose-500/10 border border-rose-500/20 p-5">
            <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-rose-200 mb-2">SSRF blocklist &amp; redirects</h2>
            <p class="text-gray-300 mb-3 text-sm">
                Every URL passed to <code class="text-amber-400">HTTP.*</code> is validated up-front: schemes other than <code class="text-amber-400">http</code>/<code class="text-amber-400">https</code> are rejected, as are hosts that resolve to loopback / private / link-local IP ranges. A request to <code class="text-amber-400">http://169.254.169.254/...</code> (cloud metadata) or <code class="text-amber-400">http://10.0.0.1/</code> fails immediately. The blocklist also covers <code class="text-amber-400">0.0.0.0/8</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">192.0.0.0/24</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">198.18.0.0/15</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">240.0.0.0/4</code>, and IPv6 addresses that embed a blocked IPv4 &mdash; NAT64 (<code class="text-amber-400">64:ff9b::/96</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">64:ff9b:1::/48</code>), 6to4 (<code class="text-amber-400">2002::/16</code>), Teredo and IPv4-compatible forms.
            </p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 text-sm">
                Redirects are followed (up to 10 hops) by every <code class="text-amber-400">HTTP.*</code> call, <code class="text-amber-400">HTTP.request</code> included, and the SSRF check runs again on each hop: a 3xx whose <code class="text-amber-400">Location</code> points at a blocked address fails with <code class="text-amber-400">SSRF: redirect target rejected: &hellip;</code> instead of being followed. An 11th hop fails with <code class="text-amber-400">too many redirects (max 10)</code>. Pass <code class="text-amber-400">"follow_redirects": false</code> to get the 3xx itself back instead.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Connections & error messages -->
    <section id="section-connections" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Connections &amp; error messages</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-3">
            Connections are <strong class="text-white">pooled and reused</strong> across calls, per host, for 15 seconds of idle time &mdash; a second request to an API you just called skips the TCP and TLS handshake. Pooling behaves the same for HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 hosts.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-400">
            A transport failure reports its <strong class="text-white">whole cause chain</strong>, not just the top line: <code class="text-amber-400">Request failed: error sending request for url (…)</code> is followed by what actually went wrong (<code class="text-amber-400">dns error: …</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">connection closed before message completed</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">invalid peer certificate</code>). Worth surfacing wherever you handle the <code class="text-amber-400">{"error": ...}</code> hashes the parallel helpers return.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mt-3">
            <strong class="text-white">A failed request raises at the call.</strong> In a controller, a job or a spec the request blocks, so <code class="text-amber-400">rescue</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">try</code>/<code class="text-amber-400">catch</code> around it catch the failure. A plain script runs requests asynchronously (the result is a Future, settled on first use); a postfix <code class="text-amber-400">rescue</code> on the call settles it on the spot, so <code class="text-amber-400">HTTP.request("GET", url) rescue nil</code> catches the failure everywhere. In a script, a <code class="text-amber-400">try</code> block does not &mdash; read the response inside it, or use <code class="text-amber-400">rescue</code>.
        </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Basic HTTP Requests -->
    <section id="section-basic-requests" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4 flex items-center gap-3">
            <span class="w-8 h-8 rounded-lg bg-green-500/20 flex items-center justify-center">
                <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-green-400" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                    <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M21 12a9 9 0 01-9 9m9-9a9 9 0 00-9-9m9 9H3m9 9a9 9 0 01-9-9m9 9c1.657 0 3-4.03 3-9s-1.343-9-3-9m0 18c-1.657 0-3-4.03-3-9s1.343-9 3-9m-9 9a9 9 0 019-9" />
                </svg>
            </span>
            Basic HTTP Requests
        </h2>

        <section id="def-http-get" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-6">
            <a href="#def-http-get" class="group flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
                <code class="text-lg font-mono text-amber-400">HTTP.get(url, options?)</code>
                <svg class="w-4 h-4 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 text-gray-500 transition-opacity" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                    <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13.828 10.172a4 4 0 00-5.656 0l-4 4a4 4 0 105.656 5.656l1.102-1.101m-.758-4.899a4 4 0 005.656 0l4-4a4 4 0 00-5.656-5.656l-1.1 1.1" />
                </svg>
            </a>
            <p class="text-gray-400 mb-3">Perform an HTTP GET request. Returns the response body as a String (in a plain script, a Future that settles to it on first use). A non-2xx response <strong>raises</strong> <code class="text-amber-400">HTTP &lt;status&gt; error: &lt;body&gt;</code>; to read the status of a 4xx/5xx instead, use <a href="#def-http-request" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">HTTP.request</a>. <code class="text-amber-400">put</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">patch</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">post</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">delete</code> behave the same way.</p>
            <pre data-filename="Example"><code class="language-soli text-sm">body = HTTP.get("https://api.example.com/users")   # String; raises on 4xx/5xx
println(body)

page = HTTP.get("https://api.example.com/maybe-missing") rescue nil

# Request headers ride along in the options hash.
me = HTTP.get("https://api.example.com/me", {
  "headers": { "Authorization": "Bearer " + token, "X-Client": "soli" }
})

# Per-call timeout in seconds (overrides the 30s default for this request).
resp = HTTP.get("https://api.example.com/slow", { "timeout": 5 })</code></pre>
            <div class="mt-3 rounded-lg border border-amber-500/30 bg-amber-500/5 p-4 text-sm text-gray-300">
                <strong class="text-amber-300">One options hash for every verb.</strong>
                <code class="text-amber-400">get</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">post</code>,
                <code class="text-amber-400">put</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">patch</code>,
                <code class="text-amber-400">delete</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">head</code>, the
                <code class="text-amber-400">*_json</code> / <code class="text-amber-400">get_jsonp</code> variants and
                (applied to every URL) <code class="text-amber-400">get_all</code> / <code class="text-amber-400">get_all_json</code>
                all take the same trailing options hash with four keys:
                <ul class="list-disc list-inside mt-2 space-y-1 ml-2">
                    <li><code class="text-amber-400">headers</code> &mdash; a hash of <code>name =&gt; value</code>. Strings go out as-is, numbers and booleans are stringified, a <code>null</code> value skips that header. A header you set <strong>replaces</strong> the builtin's own default (<code class="text-amber-400">Content-Type</code> on <code class="text-amber-400">post</code>/<code class="text-amber-400">put</code>/<code class="text-amber-400">patch</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">Accept</code> on the JSON variants), matched case-insensitively, so you never send two. A name or value the client would refuse (spaces in the name, a CR/LF in the value) raises before anything is sent &mdash; the message names the header but never echoes the value, so a rejected token stays out of the error page and logs. <code class="text-amber-400">Content-Length</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">Transfer-Encoding</code> are refused outright: they are derived from the body, and a caller-supplied length would be sent verbatim and desync the upstream connection. <strong>Redirects:</strong> the client drops <code class="text-amber-400">Authorization</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">Cookie</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">Proxy-Authorization</code> when a redirect changes host or port, but any <em>other</em> header you set (an <code class="text-amber-400">X-Api-Key</code>, say) follows the redirect to the new host.</li>
                    <li><code class="text-amber-400">timeout</code> &mdash; per-call timeout in <strong>seconds</strong> (<code class="text-amber-400">Int</code> or <code class="text-amber-400">Float</code>). Overrides the default 30s client timeout for that one request; must be positive, fractions such as <code class="text-amber-400">0.5</code> are allowed.</li>
                    <li><code class="text-amber-400">follow_redirects</code> &mdash; <code class="text-amber-400">Bool</code>, default <code class="text-amber-400">true</code>. <code class="text-amber-400">false</code> returns the first answer as-is instead of following a 3xx: no hop is made, so you read the <code class="text-amber-400">location</code> header and decide yourself. With the verb helpers a 3xx is then a non-2xx and raises (<code class="text-amber-400">HTTP 302 error: &hellip;</code>) &mdash; use <code class="text-amber-400">HTTP.request</code> to read it. Anything but a Bool raises (<code class="text-amber-400">"false"</code> is a String). Not available on the Cloudflare edge build, whose <code>fetch</code> always follows: <code class="text-amber-400">false</code> raises there.</li>
                    <li><code class="text-amber-400">response</code> &mdash; how the body comes back: <code class="text-amber-400">"text"</code> (default, UTF-8, raises otherwise), <code class="text-amber-400">"binary"</code> (Array of Ints 0&ndash;255), <code class="text-amber-400">"base64"</code> (String) or a charset label such as <code class="text-amber-400">"latin1"</code>. See <a href="#section-response-bodies" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">Response bodies</a>.</li>
                </ul>
                For <code class="text-amber-400">HTTP.request</code>, the third argument is a flat headers hash: its
                <code class="text-amber-400">timeout</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">follow_redirects</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">response</code> keys are consumed rather than sent as headers, and a nested
                <code class="text-amber-400">headers</code> hash is merged in, so the same options shape works there too. Flat and nested pairs go through the same validation.
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="def-http-get-jsonp" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-6">
            <a href="#def-http-get-jsonp" class="group flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
                <code class="text-lg font-mono text-amber-400">HTTP.get_jsonp(url, options?)</code>
                <svg class="w-4 h-4 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 text-gray-500 transition-opacity" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                    <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13.828 10.172a4 4 0 00-5.656 0l-4 4a4 4 0 105.656 5.656l1.102-1.101m-.758-4.899a4 4 0 005.656 0l4-4a4 4 0 00-5.656-5.656l-1.1 1.1" />
                </svg>
            </a>
            <p class="text-gray-400 mb-3">Fetch a JSONP endpoint and unwrap the <code class="text-amber-400">callback(...)</code> padding, returning the parsed value. Use it to consume legacy cross-origin APIs that only expose JSONP; put the <code class="text-amber-400">?callback=...</code> name in the URL you pass.</p>
            <pre data-filename="Example"><code class="language-soli text-sm">feed = HTTP.get_jsonp("https://api.example.com/feed?callback=cb")
println(feed["items"][0])</code></pre>
        </section>

        <section id="def-http-post" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-6">
            <a href="#def-http-post" class="group flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
                <code class="text-lg font-mono text-amber-400">HTTP.post(url, body, options?)</code>
                <svg class="w-4 h-4 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 text-gray-500 transition-opacity" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                    <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13.828 10.172a4 4 0 00-5.656 0l-4 4a4 4 0 105.656 5.656l1.102-1.101m-.758-4.899a4 4 0 005.656 0l4-4a4 4 0 00-5.656-5.656l-1.1 1.1" />
                </svg>
            </a>
            <p class="text-gray-400 mb-3">Perform an HTTP POST request with a body. A hash body is sent as JSON, a string body as <code class="text-amber-400">text/plain</code>, a byte array as <code class="text-amber-400">application/octet-stream</code>; a <code class="text-amber-400">Content-Type</code> in <code class="text-amber-400">options.headers</code> replaces that default. <code class="text-amber-400">"multipart": true</code> in the options sends the hash as a form &mdash; see <a href="#section-request-bodies" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">Request bodies</a>.</p>
            <pre data-filename="Example"><code class="language-soli text-sm">resp = HTTP.post(
  "https://api.example.com/users",
  "name=Alice",
  { "headers": { "Content-Type": "application/x-www-form-urlencoded" } }
)</code></pre>
        </section>

        <section id="def-http-put" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-6">
            <a href="#def-http-put" class="group flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
                <code class="text-lg font-mono text-amber-400">HTTP.put(url, body, options?)</code>
                <svg class="w-4 h-4 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 text-gray-500 transition-opacity" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                    <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13.828 10.172a4 4 0 00-5.656 0l-4 4a4 4 0 105.656 5.656l1.102-1.101m-.758-4.899a4 4 0 005.656 0l4-4a4 4 0 00-5.656-5.656l-1.1 1.1" />
                </svg>
            </a>
            <p class="text-gray-400 mb-3">Perform an HTTP PUT request with a body.</p>
            <pre data-filename="Example"><code class="language-soli text-sm">resp = HTTP.put(
  "https://api.example.com/users/1",
  { "name": "Alice Updated" }
)</code></pre>
        </section>

        <section id="def-http-delete" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-6">
            <a href="#def-http-delete" class="group flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
                <code class="text-lg font-mono text-amber-400">HTTP.delete(url, options?)</code>
                <svg class="w-4 h-4 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 text-gray-500 transition-opacity" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                    <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13.828 10.172a4 4 0 00-5.656 0l-4 4a4 4 0 105.656 5.656l1.102-1.101m-.758-4.899a4 4 0 005.656 0l4-4a4 4 0 00-5.656-5.656l-1.1 1.1" />
                </svg>
            </a>
            <p class="text-gray-400 mb-3">Perform an HTTP DELETE request.</p>
            <pre data-filename="Example"><code class="language-soli text-sm">resp = HTTP.delete("https://api.example.com/users/1")</code></pre>
        </section>

        <section id="def-http-patch" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-6">
            <a href="#def-http-patch" class="group flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
                <code class="text-lg font-mono text-amber-400">HTTP.patch(url, body, options?)</code>
                <svg class="w-4 h-4 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 text-gray-500 transition-opacity" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                    <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13.828 10.172a4 4 0 00-5.656 0l-4 4a4 4 0 105.656 5.656l1.102-1.101m-.758-4.899a4 4 0 005.656 0l4-4a4 4 0 00-5.656-5.656l-1.1 1.1" />
                </svg>
            </a>
            <p class="text-gray-400 mb-3">Perform an HTTP PATCH request with a body.</p>
            <pre data-filename="Example"><code class="language-soli text-sm">resp = HTTP.patch(
  "https://api.example.com/users/1",
  { "email": "new_email@example.com" }
)</code></pre>
        </section>

        <section id="def-http-head" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-6">
            <a href="#def-http-head" class="group flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
                <code class="text-lg font-mono text-amber-400">HTTP.head(url, options?)</code>
                <svg class="w-4 h-4 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 text-gray-500 transition-opacity" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                    <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13.828 10.172a4 4 0 00-5.656 0l-4 4a4 4 0 105.656 5.656l1.102-1.101m-.758-4.899a4 4 0 005.656 0l4-4a4 4 0 00-5.656-5.656l-1.1 1.1" />
                </svg>
            </a>
            <p class="text-gray-400 mb-3">Perform an HTTP HEAD request. Returns the status line as a String.</p>
            <pre data-filename="Example"><code class="language-soli text-sm">status_line = HTTP.head("https://api.example.com/users")
println(status_line)   # "200 OK"</code></pre>
        </section>

        <section id="def-http-download" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-6">
            <a href="#def-http-download" class="group flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
                <code class="text-lg font-mono text-amber-400">HTTP.download(url, path, options?)</code>
                <svg class="w-4 h-4 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 text-gray-500 transition-opacity" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                    <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13.828 10.172a4 4 0 00-5.656 0l-4 4a4 4 0 105.656 5.656l1.102-1.101m-.758-4.899a4 4 0 005.656 0l4-4a4 4 0 00-5.656-5.656l-1.1 1.1" />
                </svg>
            </a>
            <p class="text-gray-400 mb-3">
                Fetch a URL and write the body to a file, returning the number of bytes written.
                The one method here that does <strong>not</strong> decode: every other call ends in
                UTF-8, which a picture, a sound or a PDF is not. Same SSRF check, same
                <code class="text-amber-400">SOLI_HTTP_MAX_RESPONSE_BYTES</code> cap, and the path
                goes through the same jail as the <code class="text-amber-400">File</code> builtins
                &mdash; a missing parent directory is created, an escaping path is refused, and a
                symlink standing where the file should go is not followed. Blocking: it returns when
                the file is on disk.
            </p>
            <pre data-filename="Example"><code class="language-soli text-sm">bytes = HTTP.download("https://example.com/cover.jpg", "public/covers/1.jpg")
println("wrote " + str(bytes))</code></pre>
        </section>
    </section>

    <!-- Request bodies: bytes and multipart -->
    <section id="section-request-bodies" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Request bodies: bytes &amp; multipart</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            <code class="text-amber-400">HTTP.post</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">HTTP.put</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">HTTP.patch</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">HTTP.request</code> take the same body shapes:
        </p>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
            <table class="w-full text-sm">
                <thead>
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                        <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Body</th>
                        <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Sent as</th>
                        <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Default <code>Content-Type</code></th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5 text-gray-400">
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4">String</td><td class="py-3 px-4">the text, as-is</td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>text/plain</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4">Hash</td><td class="py-3 px-4">JSON</td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>application/json</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4">Array of Ints 0&ndash;255</td><td class="py-3 px-4">the raw bytes</td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>application/octet-stream</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4">Hash + <code>"multipart": true</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>multipart/form-data</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">set automatically, with the boundary</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-3">Binary bodies</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-3">
            A byte array is what <code class="text-amber-400">slurp(path, "binary")</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">Base64.decode</code> return, so a binary file or a decoded payload goes out byte for byte (an Int outside 0&ndash;255 raises):
        </p>
        <pre data-filename="Example"><code class="language-soli text-sm">pdf = slurp("tmp/report.pdf", "binary")
HTTP.put("https://storage.example.com/reports/q3.pdf", pdf, {
  "headers": { "Content-Type": "application/pdf" }
})</code></pre>

        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mt-6 mb-3">Multipart forms</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-3">With <code class="text-amber-400">"multipart": true</code> in the options, each key of the body hash becomes a form part:</p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-gray-400 mb-4 space-y-1">
            <li>a String, number or bool is a text part;</li>
            <li>an Array is the same field repeated (<code class="text-amber-400">"tags[]": ["a", "b"]</code>); a <code class="text-amber-400">nil</code> value is skipped;</li>
            <li>a Hash is a file part, with exactly one of <code class="text-amber-400">"path"</code> (read from disk; the filename defaults to its basename), <code class="text-amber-400">"bytes"</code> (a byte array) or <code class="text-amber-400">"content"</code> (a String), plus optional <code class="text-amber-400">"filename"</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">"content_type"</code> (default <code>application/octet-stream</code>).</li>
        </ul>
        <pre data-filename="Example"><code class="language-soli text-sm">HTTP.post("https://api.example.com/upload", {
  "title": "Q3",
  "tags[]": ["finance", "quarterly"],
  "file": { "path": "tmp/report.pdf", "content_type": "application/pdf" },
  "thumb": { "bytes": Base64.decode(thumb_b64), "filename": "thumb.png", "content_type": "image/png" }
}, { "multipart": true })</code></pre>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mt-4 mb-3">
            <code class="text-amber-400">HTTP.request</code> implies no <code>Content-Type</code> &mdash; its headers hash is the caller&rsquo;s &mdash; except for multipart, which always sets its own. There, put <code class="text-amber-400">"multipart": true</code> in the headers hash; like <code class="text-amber-400">timeout</code>, the key is consumed, not sent as a header:
        </p>
        <pre data-filename="Example"><code class="language-soli text-sm">HTTP.request("POST", url, { "Authorization": "Bearer " + token, "multipart": true }, { "file": { "path": "avatar.jpg" } })</code></pre>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-xs mt-3">Before, there was no multipart support and no way to send binary bytes: an array body was refused by <code>HTTP.post</code> and stringified by <code>HTTP.request</code>.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- Response bodies: text, bytes, base64, charset -->
    <section id="section-response-bodies" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Response bodies: text, bytes, base64, charset</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            By default a body comes back as a UTF-8 String, and a body that is not UTF-8 &mdash; a PNG, a PDF, a ZIP, an Office file, a Latin-1 page &mdash; raises <code class="text-amber-400">invalid UTF-8 in response body: &hellip; (use {"response": "binary"|"base64"} for bytes, or a charset like {"response": "latin1"})</code>. The <code class="text-amber-400">response</code> option chooses another shape. Like <code class="text-amber-400">timeout</code>, it is read and removed from the options hash, <strong>never sent as a header</strong>, and every call that returns a body takes it in the same place: <code class="text-amber-400">HTTP.get</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">post</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">put</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">patch</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">delete</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">HTTP.request</code> (in its headers hash), <code class="text-amber-400">HTTP.get_all</code> (for the whole batch), each item of <code class="text-amber-400">HTTP.parallel</code>, and <code class="text-amber-400">HTTP.stream</code> (see <code class="text-amber-400">next_chunk</code>). It is the receiving twin of <code class="text-amber-400">slurp(path, mode)</code>.
        </p>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
            <table class="w-full text-sm">
                <thead>
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                        <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold"><code>response</code></th>
                        <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold"><code>body</code> is</th>
                        <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Same as</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5 text-gray-400">
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>"text"</code> (default)</td><td class="py-3 px-4">String, UTF-8; anything else raises</td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>slurp(path)</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>"binary"</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">Array of Ints 0&ndash;255, the raw bytes</td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>slurp(path, "binary")</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>"base64"</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">String, the raw bytes base64-encoded</td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>Base64.encode(slurp(path, "binary"))</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4">a charset label (<code>"latin1"</code>, &hellip;)</td><td class="py-3 px-4">String, the bytes decoded into UTF-8</td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>slurp(path, "latin1")</code></td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-3">
            Any other value raises before anything is sent &mdash; <code class="text-amber-400">HTTP response must be "text", "binary", "base64" or a charset label like "latin1", got "bytes"</code> &mdash; as does a value that is not a String. The <code class="text-amber-400">*_json</code> helpers (<code class="text-amber-400">get_json</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">get_jsonp</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">post_json</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">put_json</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">patch_json</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">get_all_json</code>) parse the body as text: they take <code class="text-amber-400">"text"</code> or a charset and refuse <code class="text-amber-400">"binary"</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">"base64"</code>. <code class="text-amber-400">HTTP.head</code> has no body and <code class="text-amber-400">HTTP.download</code> always writes raw bytes, so both ignore it.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-3">
            <strong class="text-white">Size limit.</strong> Every mode reads through the same cap as a text body: <code class="text-amber-400">SOLI_HTTP_MAX_RESPONSE_BYTES</code>, <strong>50&nbsp;MiB (52428800 bytes) by default</strong>. A longer body raises <code class="text-amber-400">HTTP response exceeded 52428800 bytes (SOLI_HTTP_MAX_RESPONSE_BYTES)</code>; set the variable (in bytes) to raise it. The cap counts the bytes received, before any base64 or charset conversion. <code class="text-amber-400">"binary"</code> makes one Array element per byte; for a file of several MB headed to another API, <code class="text-amber-400">"base64"</code> is far lighter.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
            The error body of a verb helper&rsquo;s non-2xx (<code class="text-amber-400">HTTP 404 error: &hellip;</code>) is always read as text, whatever the mode. The option works the same on the Cloudflare edge build (which has no <code class="text-amber-400">HTTP.stream</code>).
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-3"><code>"text"</code> &mdash; the default</h3>
        <pre data-filename="Example"><code class="language-soli text-sm">html = HTTP.get("https://example.com/")            # String
same = HTTP.get("https://example.com/", {"response": "text"})</code></pre>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mt-3 mb-6">Unchanged from before: a body that is not valid UTF-8 raises, and the message names the option to use instead.</p>

        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-3"><code>"binary"</code> &mdash; the raw bytes</h3>
        <pre data-filename="Example"><code class="language-soli text-sm">response = HTTP.request("GET", attachment_url, {"Authorization": auth, "response": "binary"})
bytes = response["body"]                 # [137, 80, 78, 71, 13, 10, 26, 10, …]
barf("storage/report.pdf", bytes)        # written byte for byte
bytes == slurp("storage/report.pdf", "binary")   # true</code></pre>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mt-3 mb-6">An empty body is <code class="text-amber-400">[]</code>.</p>

        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-3"><code>"base64"</code> &mdash; the raw bytes as base64</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-3">Base64 with the standard alphabet and padding, byte-identical to <code class="text-amber-400">Base64.encode(bytes)</code> &mdash; without the <code class="text-amber-400">Array&lt;Int&gt;</code> in between, so a several-megabyte attachment costs its size and the base64 text, not one Soli value per byte:</p>
        <pre data-filename="Example"><code class="language-soli text-sm"># Download a Jira attachment and forward it to another API.
content = HTTP.get(attachment["content"], {"headers": {"Authorization": auth}, "response": "base64"})
HTTP.post_json(upload_url, {"filename": attachment["filename"], "data": content})</code></pre>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mt-3 mb-6">An empty body is <code class="text-amber-400">""</code>.</p>

        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-3">A charset &mdash; decoded into UTF-8</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-3">Any label the <a href="https://encoding.spec.whatwg.org/#names-and-labels" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">WHATWG Encoding Standard</a> knows, as <code class="text-amber-400">Encoding.decode</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">slurp(path, label)</code> take it: <code class="text-amber-400">"latin1"</code> / <code class="text-amber-400">"iso-8859-1"</code> (both read as <code>windows-1252</code>, a superset), <code class="text-amber-400">"windows-1252"</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">"iso-8859-15"</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">"shift_jis"</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">"euc-jp"</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">"gbk"</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">"big5"</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">"koi8-r"</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">"utf-16le"</code>, &hellip; Labels are case-insensitive. <code class="text-amber-400">"utf-8"</code> is accepted too and is the lenient form of <code class="text-amber-400">"text"</code>: an invalid sequence becomes U+FFFD instead of raising.</p>
        <pre data-filename="Example"><code class="language-soli text-sm">page = HTTP.get("https://legacy.example.com/", {"response": "latin1"})   # "café", not an error</code></pre>
    </section>

    <!-- JSON HTTP Methods -->
    <section id="section-json-methods" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">JSON HTTP Methods</h2>

        <section id="def-http-get-json" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-6">
            <a href="#def-http-get-json" class="group flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
                <code class="text-lg font-mono text-amber-400">HTTP.get_json(url, options?)</code>
                <svg class="w-4 h-4 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 text-gray-500 transition-opacity" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                    <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13.828 10.172a4 4 0 00-5.656 0l-4 4a4 4 0 105.656 5.656l1.102-1.101m-.758-4.899a4 4 0 005.656 0l4-4a4 4 0 00-5.656-5.656l-1.1 1.1" />
                </svg>
            </a>
            <p class="text-gray-400 mb-3">GET request with automatic JSON parsing of response body. Returns the parsed body itself (Hash, Array, &hellip;) &mdash; no status/headers wrapper &mdash; and raises <code class="text-amber-400">HTTP &lt;status&gt; error: &lt;body&gt;</code> on a non-2xx response. Sends <code class="text-amber-400">Accept: application/json</code> unless <code class="text-amber-400">options.headers</code> overrides it.</p>
            <pre data-filename="Example"><code class="language-soli text-sm">user = HTTP.get_json("https://api.example.com/users/1")
println(user["name"])

# Authenticated, with a 10-second budget.
me = HTTP.get_json("https://api.example.com/me", {
  "headers": { "Authorization": "Bearer " + token },
  "timeout": 10
})</code></pre>
        </section>

        <section id="def-http-post-json" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-6">
            <a href="#def-http-post-json" class="group flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
                <code class="text-lg font-mono text-amber-400">HTTP.post_json(url, data, options?)</code>
                <svg class="w-4 h-4 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 text-gray-500 transition-opacity" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                    <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13.828 10.172a4 4 0 00-5.656 0l-4 4a4 4 0 105.656 5.656l1.102-1.101m-.758-4.899a4 4 0 005.656 0l4-4a4 4 0 00-5.656-5.656l-1.1 1.1" />
                </svg>
            </a>
            <p class="text-gray-400 mb-3">POST request with automatic JSON serialization. Returns the parsed JSON response body; raises on a non-2xx response. <code class="text-amber-400">put_json</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">patch_json</code> do the same.</p>
            <pre data-filename="Example"><code class="language-soli text-sm">resp = HTTP.post_json(
  "https://api.example.com/users",
  { "name": "Alice", "email": "alice@example.com" }
)</code></pre>
        </section>

        <section id="def-http-put-json" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-6">
            <a href="#def-http-put-json" class="group flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
                <code class="text-lg font-mono text-amber-400">HTTP.put_json(url, data, options?)</code>
                <svg class="w-4 h-4 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 text-gray-500 transition-opacity" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                    <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13.828 10.172a4 4 0 00-5.656 0l-4 4a4 4 0 105.656 5.656l1.102-1.101m-.758-4.899a4 4 0 005.656 0l4-4a4 4 0 00-5.656-5.656l-1.1 1.1" />
                </svg>
            </a>
            <p class="text-gray-400 mb-3">PUT request with automatic JSON serialization.</p>
            <pre data-filename="Example"><code class="language-soli text-sm">resp = HTTP.put_json(
  "https://api.example.com/users/1",
  { "name": "Alice Updated", "email": "alice_updated@example.com" }
)</code></pre>
        </section>

        <section id="def-http-patch-json" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-6">
            <a href="#def-http-patch-json" class="group flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
                <code class="text-lg font-mono text-amber-400">HTTP.patch_json(url, data, options?)</code>
                <svg class="w-4 h-4 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 text-gray-500 transition-opacity" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                    <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13.828 10.172a4 4 0 00-5.656 0l-4 4a4 4 0 105.656 5.656l1.102-1.101m-.758-4.899a4 4 0 005.656 0l4-4a4 4 0 00-5.656-5.656l-1.1 1.1" />
                </svg>
            </a>
            <p class="text-gray-400 mb-3">PATCH request with automatic JSON serialization.</p>
            <pre data-filename="Example"><code class="language-soli text-sm">resp = HTTP.patch_json(
  "https://api.example.com/users/1",
  { "email": "new_email@example.com" }
)</code></pre>
        </section>
    </section>

    <!-- Generic HTTP Request -->
    <section id="section-generic-request" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Generic HTTP Request</h2>

        <section id="def-http-request" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-6">
            <a href="#def-http-request" class="group flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
                <code class="text-lg font-mono text-amber-400">HTTP.request(method, url, headers?, body?)</code>
                <svg class="w-4 h-4 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 text-gray-500 transition-opacity" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                    <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13.828 10.172a4 4 0 00-5.656 0l-4 4a4 4 0 105.656 5.656l1.102-1.101m-.758-4.899a4 4 0 005.656 0l4-4a4 4 0 00-5.656-5.656l-1.1 1.1" />
                </svg>
            </a>
            <p class="text-gray-400 mb-3">Perform any HTTP method (GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE, etc.). The headers hash accepts a <code class="text-amber-400">timeout</code> key (seconds) that is consumed as the per-call timeout rather than sent as a header. The body takes the same shapes as <code class="text-amber-400">HTTP.post</code> (string, hash as JSON, byte array, or multipart with <code class="text-amber-400">"multipart": true</code> in the headers hash), but no <code>Content-Type</code> is implied unless it is multipart.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-400 mb-3">Returns <code class="text-amber-400">{ "status", "status_text", "headers", "body" }</code> (<code class="text-amber-400">body</code> an Array of bytes with <code class="text-amber-400">"response": "binary"</code> &mdash; see <a href="#section-response-bodies" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">Response bodies</a>). Unlike the verb helpers it does <strong>not</strong> raise on a 4xx/5xx &mdash; branch on <code class="text-amber-400">resp["status"]</code>. Only a transport failure (DNS, refused connection, timeout, SSRF refusal) raises. <code class="text-amber-400">HTTP.parallel</code> returns one such hash per request.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-400 mb-3"><strong class="text-white">Response headers.</strong> Names are lowercase. <code class="text-amber-400">set-cookie</code> is <strong>always an Array of strings</strong>, one per <code class="text-amber-400">Set-Cookie</code> line, even when the server sent a single cookie (a cookie&rsquo;s <code class="text-amber-400">Expires</code> attribute holds a comma, so the lines cannot be joined). Any other header sent more than once is one String, its values joined with <code class="text-amber-400">", "</code> (<code class="text-amber-400">"vary" =&gt; "accept, origin"</code>). The same shape holds for <code class="text-amber-400">HTTP.parallel</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">stream.headers</code> from <code class="text-amber-400">HTTP.stream</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">SOAP.call</code>; <code class="text-amber-400">res_header(resp, "set-cookie")</code> returns the same Array.</p>
            <pre data-filename="Example"><code class="language-soli text-sm">resp = HTTP.request("POST", login_url, {}, credentials)
resp["headers"]["set-cookie"]   # ["sid=abc; HttpOnly", "theme=dark; Path=/"]
resp["headers"]["set-cookie"].each do |line|
  print(line.split(";")[0])     # "sid=abc", then "theme=dark"
end</code></pre>
            <pre data-filename="Example"><code class="language-soli text-sm">resp = HTTP.request("DELETE", "https://api.example.com/users/1")
println("gone") if resp["status"] == 404
resp = HTTP.request("PATCH", url, headers, json)

# Custom header plus a 3-second per-call timeout.
resp = HTTP.request("GET", url, { "Authorization": "Bearer " + token, "timeout": 3 })</code></pre>
            <p class="text-gray-400 mb-3"><strong class="text-white">Handling a redirect yourself.</strong> <code class="text-amber-400">"follow_redirects": false</code> stops at the first answer: a 301/302/303/307/308 comes back with its status, its body and its <code class="text-amber-400">location</code> header, and nothing else is requested &mdash; for a login flow that must keep the <code class="text-amber-400">set-cookie</code> of the 302, or to check where a link leads before going there.</p>
            <pre data-filename="Example"><code class="language-soli text-sm">resp = HTTP.request("GET", "https://example.com/short/abc", { "follow_redirects": false })
if [301, 302, 303, 307, 308].includes?(resp["status"])
  target = resp["headers"]["location"]   # as the server sent it: may be relative
end</code></pre>
        </section>

        <section id="def-http-stream" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-6">
            <a href="#def-http-stream" class="group flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
                <code class="text-lg font-mono text-amber-400">HTTP.stream(method, url, options?, body?)</code>
                <svg class="w-4 h-4 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 text-gray-500 transition-opacity" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                    <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13.828 10.172a4 4 0 00-5.656 0l-4 4a4 4 0 105.656 5.656l1.102-1.101m-.758-4.899a4 4 0 005.656 0l4-4a4 4 0 00-5.656-5.656l-1.1 1.1" />
                </svg>
            </a>
            <p class="text-gray-400 mb-3">Opens a request and answers as soon as its headers are in, with an object the body is read from one line at a time, as it arrives: an AI model answering in server-sent events, a long export, a log tail. <code class="text-amber-400">HTTP.request</code> waits for the whole body, and its timeout counts from the start; a stream runs as long as it keeps sending.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-400 mb-3">Parameters are <code class="text-amber-400">HTTP.request</code>&rsquo;s, <code class="text-amber-400">follow_redirects</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">response</code> included. The <code class="text-amber-400">timeout</code> (seconds, default 120) is the longest wait <strong>for the next bytes</strong>, not for the whole answer.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-400 mb-3">Returns an <code class="text-amber-400">HTTPStream</code>:</p>
            <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-gray-400 mb-3 space-y-1">
                <li><code class="text-amber-400">stream.status</code> (Int) and <code class="text-amber-400">stream.headers</code> (Hash, <code class="text-amber-400">HTTP.request</code>&rsquo;s shape: <code class="text-amber-400">set-cookie</code> an Array), read when it opened;</li>
                <li><code class="text-amber-400">stream.next_line</code>: the next line, with its <code>\n</code> (and a <code>\r</code> before it) taken off, or <code class="text-amber-400">nil</code> once the body is over (an unended last line comes first). It raises when nothing arrives for <code class="text-amber-400">timeout</code> seconds, when the connection breaks, and past <code class="text-amber-400">SOLI_HTTP_MAX_RESPONSE_BYTES</code>; the stream is closed then;</li>
                <li><code class="text-amber-400">stream.next_chunk</code>: the next bytes <strong>as they came</strong>, with no line splitting and nothing taken off (<code>\r\n</code> included) &mdash; an Array of Ints 0&ndash;255, or a base64 String when the stream was opened with <code class="text-amber-400">{"response": "base64"}</code>; <code class="text-amber-400">nil</code> once the body is over. Same idle timeout and size limit as <code class="text-amber-400">next_line</code>. Each base64 chunk is encoded on its own: decode them one by one rather than concatenating the strings;</li>
                <li><code class="text-amber-400">stream.close</code> lets the connection go before the end.</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="text-gray-400 mb-3"><code class="text-amber-400">next_line</code> decodes each line as UTF-8 (an invalid sequence becomes U+FFFD), or from the charset given as <code class="text-amber-400">{"response": "latin1"}</code>. Use <code class="text-amber-400">next_chunk</code> for a binary download read piece by piece. <code class="text-amber-400">HTTP.stream_to</code> reads text events and refuses the <code class="text-amber-400">response</code> option.</p>
            <pre data-filename="Example"><code class="language-soli text-sm">stream = HTTP.stream("GET", file_url, {"Authorization": auth, "response": "binary"})
received = 0
chunk = stream.next_chunk
while !chunk.nil?
  received = received + chunk.length
  chunk = stream.next_chunk
end</code></pre>
            <p class="text-gray-400 mb-3">A non-2xx status does not raise: check <code class="text-amber-400">stream.status</code>, its body is readable. The SSRF check, the header checks and the redirect policy are <code class="text-amber-400">HTTP.request</code>&rsquo;s. The stream is read in a loop rather than with <code class="text-amber-400">do |line|</code>: plain natives cannot call a block.</p>
            <pre data-filename="Example"><code class="language-soli text-sm">stream = HTTP.stream("POST", "https://api.example.com/v1/chat/completions",
  {"Authorization": "Bearer " + key, "content-type": "application/json", "timeout": 120},
  JSON.stringify({"model": model, "stream": true, "messages": messages}))
raise "refused: #{stream.status}" unless stream.status == 200
text = ""
line = stream.next_line
while !line.nil?
  if line.starts_with?("data: ") &amp;&amp; line != "data: [DONE]"
    delta = JSON.parse(line.slice(6, line.length))["choices"][0]["delta"]["content"]
    text = text + delta.to_s
  end
  line = stream.next_line
end</code></pre>
        </section>

        <section id="def-http-stream-to" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-6">
            <a href="#def-http-stream-to" class="group flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
                <code class="text-lg font-mono text-amber-400">HTTP.stream_to(method, url, options?, body?, to)</code>
                <svg class="w-4 h-4 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 text-gray-500 transition-opacity" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                    <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13.828 10.172a4 4 0 00-5.656 0l-4 4a4 4 0 105.656 5.656l1.102-1.101m-.758-4.899a4 4 0 005.656 0l4-4a4 4 0 00-5.656-5.656l-1.1 1.1" />
                </svg>
            </a>
            <p class="text-gray-400 mb-3">Streams an answer <strong class="text-white">without holding a thread</strong>. <code class="text-amber-400">HTTP.stream</code> hands each line to Soli code, so the thread reading it waits as long as the answer takes: a job that streams a model&rsquo;s ten-minute answer keeps a job worker for ten minutes. <code class="text-amber-400">HTTP.stream_to</code> returns an id at once and leaves the reading to the HTTP runtime. It takes the text out of each event, sends it to the pages subscribed to a topic, and hands the whole answer to a job at the end. No Soli code runs and no thread waits in between.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-400 mb-3">The first four parameters are <code class="text-amber-400">HTTP.stream</code>&rsquo;s (<code class="text-amber-400">timeout</code> is the wait for the next bytes, default 120). <code class="text-amber-400">to</code> is a hash:</p>
            <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-gray-400 mb-3 space-y-1">
                <li><code class="text-amber-400">format</code>: how the text is taken out. <code class="text-amber-400">"anthropic"</code> (Messages API events: the <code class="text-amber-400">text_delta</code>s, <code class="text-amber-400">stop_reason</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">usage</code>; an <code class="text-amber-400">error</code> event fails the stream), <code class="text-amber-400">"openai"</code> (chat completion chunks: <code class="text-amber-400">choices[0].delta.content</code> up to <code class="text-amber-400">[DONE]</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">finish_reason</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">usage</code>), <code class="text-amber-400">"sse"</code> (each event&rsquo;s data) or <code class="text-amber-400">"lines"</code> (each line; the default);</li>
                <li><code class="text-amber-400">topic</code> (optional): an <code class="text-amber-400">sse_subscribe</code> topic. Each chunk&rsquo;s new text is broadcast as a <code class="text-amber-400">delta</code> event, <code class="text-amber-400">{"id", "text"}</code>. At the end comes one <code class="text-amber-400">done</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">failed</code> or <code class="text-amber-400">cancelled</code> event, <code class="text-amber-400">{"id", "text", "error"?}</code>;</li>
                <li><code class="text-amber-400">job</code> (optional): a job class, enqueued once when the stream ends, failed or cancelled ones included;</li>
                <li><code class="text-amber-400">args</code> (optional hash): passed to the job, merged with <code class="text-amber-400">stream_id</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">state</code> (<code class="text-amber-400">"done"</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">"failed"</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">"cancelled"</code>), <code class="text-amber-400">status</code> (the HTTP code), <code class="text-amber-400">text</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">error</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">stop_reason</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">usage</code>.</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="text-gray-400 mb-3">Returns the stream&rsquo;s id, a string. A bad URL, option or <code class="text-amber-400">to</code> raises at once. Whatever goes wrong afterwards (no answer, a 4xx/5xx with its body as <code class="text-amber-400">error</code>, a broken connection, more than <code class="text-amber-400">SOLI_HTTP_MAX_RESPONSE_BYTES</code>) ends the stream as <code class="text-amber-400">failed</code>, and the job still runs.</p>
            <pre data-filename="app/controllers/answers_controller.sl"><code class="language-soli text-sm">class AnswersController &lt; Controller
  # POST /chats/:id/answer — starts the answer and returns at once
  def answer(req)
    chat = Chat.find(params["id"])
    HTTP.stream_to("POST", "https://api.anthropic.com/v1/messages",
      {"x-api-key": getenv("ANTHROPIC_API_KEY"), "anthropic-version": "2023-06-01", "timeout": 600},
      {"model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "max_tokens": 4096, "stream": true,
       "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": chat.question}]},
      {"format": "anthropic", "topic": "chat:#{chat._key}", "job": "AnswerDoneJob", "args": {"chat": chat._key}})
    render_json({"started": true})
  end

  # GET /chats/:id/stream — the page's EventSource
  def stream(req)
    sse_subscribe(req, "chat:#{params["id"]}")
  end
end</code></pre>
            <pre data-filename="app/jobs/answer_done_job.sl"><code class="language-soli text-sm">class AnswerDoneJob
  static def perform(args)
    Chat.find(args["chat"]).update({"answer": args["text"], "state": args["state"]})
  end
end</code></pre>
            <pre data-filename="In the page"><code class="language-javascript text-sm">const es = new EventSource(`/chats/${id}/stream`)
es.addEventListener("delta", e =&gt; { out.textContent += JSON.parse(e.data).text })
es.addEventListener("done", () =&gt; es.close())</code></pre>
            <p class="text-gray-400 mt-3 mb-3"><strong class="text-white">Limits.</strong> A stream lives in the process that started it. If that process stops, the stream is lost: it is not resumed and its job is not enqueued. <code class="text-amber-400">HTTP.stream_status</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">HTTP.stream_cancel</code> only see this process&rsquo;s streams, so with several servers, follow a stream through its <code class="text-amber-400">topic</code> and its <code class="text-amber-400">job</code>. At most <code class="text-amber-400">SOLI_HTTP_MAX_STREAMS</code> (256) run at once. A plain <code class="text-amber-400">soli script.sl</code> waits for its streams before it exits, up to <code class="text-amber-400">SOLI_HTTP_STREAM_EXIT_WAIT</code> seconds when that is set.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="def-http-stream-status" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-6">
            <a href="#def-http-stream-status" class="group flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
                <code class="text-lg font-mono text-amber-400">HTTP.stream_status(id)</code>
                <svg class="w-4 h-4 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 text-gray-500 transition-opacity" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                    <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13.828 10.172a4 4 0 00-5.656 0l-4 4a4 4 0 105.656 5.656l1.102-1.101m-.758-4.899a4 4 0 005.656 0l4-4a4 4 0 00-5.656-5.656l-1.1 1.1" />
                </svg>
            </a>
            <p class="text-gray-400 mb-3">Where a <code class="text-amber-400">stream_to</code> stream stands, or <code class="text-amber-400">nil</code> for an id this process does not know. A finished stream is kept for ten minutes. Returns <code class="text-amber-400">{"id", "state", "text", "status", "error", "stop_reason"}</code>: <code class="text-amber-400">state</code> is <code class="text-amber-400">"running"</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">"done"</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">"failed"</code> or <code class="text-amber-400">"cancelled"</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">text</code> the text so far, and <code class="text-amber-400">status</code> the HTTP code (<code class="text-amber-400">nil</code> before the headers come).</p>
        </section>

        <section id="def-http-stream-cancel" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-6">
            <a href="#def-http-stream-cancel" class="group flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
                <code class="text-lg font-mono text-amber-400">HTTP.stream_cancel(id)</code>
                <svg class="w-4 h-4 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 text-gray-500 transition-opacity" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                    <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13.828 10.172a4 4 0 00-5.656 0l-4 4a4 4 0 105.656 5.656l1.102-1.101m-.758-4.899a4 4 0 005.656 0l4-4a4 4 0 00-5.656-5.656l-1.1 1.1" />
                </svg>
            </a>
            <p class="text-gray-400 mb-3">Stops a running <code class="text-amber-400">stream_to</code> stream. It ends as <code class="text-amber-400">cancelled</code>, and its job still runs with the text so far. Returns <code class="text-amber-400">true</code> if the stream was running in this process.</p>
        </section>
    </section>

    <!-- Status Code Helpers -->
    <section id="section-status-helpers" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Status Code Helpers</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-400">There are none: <code class="text-amber-400">http_ok</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">http_success</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">http_redirect</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">http_client_error</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">http_server_error</code> went with the standalone <code>http_*</code> helpers and are undefined at run time. Compare <code class="text-amber-400">resp["status"]</code> from <a href="#def-http-request" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">HTTP.request</a>, or rely on the verb helpers raising on non-2xx.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- JSON Helpers -->
    <section id="section-json-helpers" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">JSON Helpers</h2>

        <div class="rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10 p-5 mb-6">
            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
                <section id="def-http-json-parse" class="scroll-mt-20">
                    <a href="#def-http-json-parse" class="group flex items-center gap-2">
                        <code class="font-mono text-amber-400">HTTP.json_parse(string)</code>
                        <svg class="w-4 h-4 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 text-gray-500 transition-opacity" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                            <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13.828 10.172a4 4 0 00-5.656 0l-4 4a4 4 0 105.656 5.656l1.102-1.101m-.758-4.899a4 4 0 005.656 0l4-4a4 4 0 00-5.656-5.656l-1.1 1.1" />
                        </svg>
                    </a>
                    <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm mt-1">Parse a JSON string into a Soli value</p>
                </section>

                <section id="def-http-json-stringify" class="scroll-mt-20">
                    <a href="#def-http-json-stringify" class="group flex items-center gap-2">
                        <code class="font-mono text-amber-400">HTTP.json_stringify(value)</code>
                        <svg class="w-4 h-4 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 text-gray-500 transition-opacity" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                            <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13.828 10.172a4 4 0 00-5.656 0l-4 4a4 4 0 105.656 5.656l1.102-1.101m-.758-4.899a4 4 0 005.656 0l4-4a4 4 0 00-5.656-5.656l-1.1 1.1" />
                        </svg>
                    </a>
                    <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm mt-1">Convert a Soli value to a JSON string</p>
                </section>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Parallel Requests -->
    <section id="section-parallel-requests" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Parallel Requests</h2>

        <section id="def-http-get-all" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-6">
            <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 p-5">
                <a bg-white/5 href="#def-http-get-all" class="group flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
                    <code class="text-lg font-mono text-amber-400">HTTP.get_all(urls, options?)</code>
                    <svg class="w-4 h-4 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 text-gray-500 transition-opacity" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                        <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13.828 10.172a4 4 0 00-5.656 0l-4 4a4 4 0 105.656 5.656l1.102-1.101m-.758-4.899a4 4 0 005.656 0l4-4a4 4 0 00-5.656-5.656l-1.1 1.1" />
                    </svg>
                </a>
                <p class="text-gray-400 mb-3">Execute multiple GET requests in parallel. Returns response bodies as strings; failed requests appear as <code>{"error": ...}</code> hashes. An optional trailing options hash (<code class="text-amber-400">headers</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">timeout</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">follow_redirects</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">response</code> &mdash; see <a href="#def-http-get" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">HTTP.get</a>) applies to every request in the batch; with <code class="text-amber-400">"response": "binary"</code> each body is a byte Array.</p>
                <pre data-filename="Example"><code class="language-soli text-sm">responses = HTTP.get_all([
  "https://api.example.com/users",
  "https://api.example.com/posts"
])

# Cap every request in the batch at 5 seconds.
responses = HTTP.get_all([
  "https://api.example.com/users",
  "https://api.example.com/posts"
], { "timeout": 5 })</code></pre>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="def-http-get-all-json" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-6">
            <div class="rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10 p-5">
                <a href="#def-http-get-all-json" class="group flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
                    <code class="text-lg font-mono text-amber-400">HTTP.get_all_json(urls, options?)</code>
                    <svg class="w-4 h-4 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 text-gray-500 transition-opacity" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                        <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13.828 10.172a4 4 0 00-5.656 0l-4 4a4 4 0 105.656 5.656l1.102-1.101m-.758-4.899a4 4 0 005.656 0l4-4a4 4 0 00-5.656-5.656l-1.1 1.1" />
                    </svg>
                </a>
                <p class="text-gray-400 mb-3">Execute multiple GET requests in parallel and parse each body as JSON. Failed requests, non-2xx responses, or unparseable bodies appear as <code>{"error": ...}</code> hashes. Accepts the same optional trailing options hash (<code class="text-amber-400">headers</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">timeout</code>) as <code class="text-amber-400">HTTP.get_all</code>.</p>
                <pre data-filename="Example"><code class="language-soli text-sm">responses = HTTP.get_all_json([
  "https://api.example.com/users.json",
  "https://api.example.com/posts.json"
])

users = responses[0]
if users.has_key("error") {
  print("users failed: " + users["error"])
}</code></pre>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="def-http-parallel" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-6">
            <div class="rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10 p-5">
                <a href="#def-http-parallel" class="group flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
                    <code class="text-lg font-mono text-amber-400">HTTP.parallel(requests)</code>
                    <svg class="w-4 h-4 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 text-gray-500 transition-opacity" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                        <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13.828 10.172a4 4 0 00-5.656 0l-4 4a4 4 0 105.656 5.656l1.102-1.101m-.758-4.899a4 4 0 005.656 0l4-4a4 4 0 00-5.656-5.656l-1.1 1.1" />
                    </svg>
                </a>
                <p class="text-gray-400 mb-3">Execute multiple requests of different methods in parallel. Each request hash accepts <code class="text-amber-400">method</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">url</code>, optional <code class="text-amber-400">headers</code>, optional <code class="text-amber-400">body</code>, an optional per-request <code class="text-amber-400">timeout</code> (seconds), an optional per-request <code class="text-amber-400">follow_redirects</code> (Bool) and an optional per-request <code class="text-amber-400">response</code> (see <a href="#section-response-bodies" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">Response bodies</a>).</p>
                <pre data-filename="Example"><code class="language-soli text-sm">responses = HTTP.parallel([
  { "method": "GET", "url": "https://api.example.com/users", "timeout": 5 },
  { "method": "POST", "url": "https://api.example.com/logs", "body": "{}" }
])</code></pre>
            </div>
        </section>
    </section>

</div>
